      "level": 2,
      "content": "PART A: FUNDAMENTAL NATURE OF INDIAN CLIMATE"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Indian climate is governed by latitudinal location, altitude, continentality, and upper air atmospheric pressure/wind systems.",
        "The origin and mechanism of the Indian Monsoon involve thermal theory, ITCZ movement, and jet stream circulation.",
        "The Southwest Monsoon operates through two primary branches: Arabian Sea Branch and Bay of Bengal Branch.",
        "Monsoon variability and rainfall breaks are influenced by global teleconnections like El Niño – Southern Oscillation (ENSO) and Indian Ocean Dipole (IOD)."
      ],
      "id": "z9a0wf4"
    },
